// PORT-PENDING 1125 tests-package-outputs: tests/package_outputs.rs:1-826
// Translate in place: replace the //RS lines below with the 1:1 Jet port (keep `// Rust: <file>:<line>` anchors).
//RS //! D-CONF-PLANE1/D-CONF-NAME1 (card #1517): a package.jet `outputs:` block
//RS //! drives entry resolution for both `jet run` (JIT) and `jet build` (AOT) —
//RS //! I9 tier parity for the manifest-driven build path.
//RS //!
//RS //! `Examples/features/packages/outputs_build/` has no `main.jet`/`run.jet`
//RS //! convention file. Its `package.jet` uses a *dotted* entry —
//RS //! `outputs: { demo: .Executable{ entry: service.run } }` — which names the
//RS //! nested package member `service/service.jet` by its file stem
//RS //! (D-MOD-CYCLE1=A: a package is one namespace without file imports). That's
//RS //! deliberate: a single-segment `entry: run` resolves through the existing
//RS //! root-level source lookup, so it can never prove `outputs:` is doing
//RS //! anything.
//RS //! This fixture has no canonical entry and can only resolve through
//RS //! `outputs:`. `entry_resolution_requires_the_outputs_block` below proves it:
//RS //! the same fixture with `outputs:` deleted fails to resolve at all.
//RS //!
//RS //! `golden.rs`'s directory scan (which requires a `main.<ext>`) never
//RS //! discovers this fixture and always compiles a single file directly,
//RS //! bypassing package/output resolution — so this is a dedicated test, not an
//RS //! addition to that harness.
//RS 
//RS use std::fs;
//RS use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
//RS use std::process::Command;
//RS 
